		{
			"id": "117242034593047197",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR/117242034574188743",
			"author": {
				"name": "Amelia Bellamy-Royds",
				"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/304/885/279/901/783/original/ac1081e7e8ac0f19.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2026-09-09T16:31:14.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> So, to be clear: you are still building 474 open graph *images*, you're just not building 474 separate static HTML pages just to screenshot them to make the images. </p><p>You're using the Chromium-based screenshot service as the build tool instead of using eleventy plus also the screenshot service.</p><p>That seems like a sensible use of resources. But its not quite as impressive as you implied yesterday; your total build time including the screenshots is still proportional to page count.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "117242193280525431",
			"parentId": "117242034593047197",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/117242193280525431",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2026-09-09T17:11:36.492Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>AmeliaBR</span></a></span> I suppose! I don’t include the screenshots in my mental model of build times because they aren’t “blocking” build times, they happen independently as needed (but your point is valid!)</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "117242771842957624",
			"parentId": "117242193280525431",
			"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR/117242771825661368",
			"author": {
				"name": "Amelia Bellamy-Royds",
				"url": "https://front-end.social/@AmeliaBR",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/304/885/279/901/783/original/ac1081e7e8ac0f19.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2026-09-09T19:38:44.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> If the screenshot images are only generated on demand by your image caching layer, that also I guess means you're not necessarily re-building images for your entire archive of posts every time you tweak something.</p><p>But be careful about following that logic too far: you could end up re-inventing PHP just to speed up the build times on your \"static\" website!</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
